The multi-core RV64I processor system is designed with performance, scalability, and
implementation feasibility in mind. It consists of four symmetric RV64I cores, each
equipped with private instruction and data caches. These cores follow a classic 5-stage
in-order pipeline and support the base integer instruction set of the RISC-V architecture.
The memory architecture includes a unified 256KB shared memory that is accessed via a
crossbar interconnect, enabling low-latency communication between cores. The caches are
configured as 4-way set associative with a 32-byte block size and implement a write-back
policy.
This system is targeted for RTL-level simulation and FPGA prototyping using tools
such as Vivado, Verilator, and ModelSim. It supports a 64-bit data path and operates
at a simulated frequency of 100 MHz. Additional architectural features include memorymapped I/O, basic atomic operations for synchronization, and future support for cache
coherence protocols. Overall, the design is aimed at academic validation and real-time
embedded applications where performance and modularity are critical.
